<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"/><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"/><meta name="description" content="Krea Pixel Art recipes, native PNG downloads and practical tips for Atelier."/><title>Krea Pixel Art — Atelier Guide</title></head><body>
<header><a class="brand" href="/studio/">← atelier</a><span>Pixel art guide · October 31, 2026</span></header>
<main style="max-width:1100px;padding-bottom:70px"><section class="intro"><div><p class="eyebrow">SMALL GRIDS. CLEAR IDEAS.</p><h1>Build a little world.</h1><p>Choose Krea Pixel Art, pick a recipe and describe your subject. The service selects the LoRA, prepares the prompt and cleans up the pixel grid.</p><a class="quiet button" href="/studio/#explore">Choose an example ↗</a></div></section>
<section class="guide"><div><h2>Start here</h2><p>Use the recipe’s Recommended settings: 1024 × 1024, 8 Euler steps, CFG 1 and a LoRA strength of 1. Change the subject first. Keep a fixed seed while comparing a pose, color or palette.</p><p>The three DoRA adapters were trained on Krea 2 Raw. Atelier prepares a separate GGUF for each variant, including the magnitude weights; changing the grid reloads that variant. Krea recommends applying Raw-trained LoRAs to Turbo for inference; Atelier uses Krea 2 Turbo Q3_K_M on the local 8 GB GPU.</p></div><div><h3>Choose the right grid</h3><p><strong>32:</strong> large clusters, small icons and simple objects.<br/><strong>64:</strong> characters, buildings and game assets.<br/><strong>128:</strong> landscapes and scenes with smaller details.</p><p>The author recommends the trigger “pixel art” for 32 and 128, and omitting it for 64. Guided recipes handle this when you change the grid. In Free prompt, follow the same advice yourself.</p></div><div><h3>Choose a palette</h3><p>Start with 24 colors for icons and characters, or 32 for scenes. Sixteen colors give stronger abstraction; 64 or 128 retain more subtle shading. Adding colors cannot restore a missing hand or fix an unclear pose.</p><p>Describe one main subject, a clear silhouette, a simple background and a consistent light direction. For a character, ask for a complete head-to-feet view with a margin.</p></div></section>
<section aria-labelledby="examples-title"><h2 id="examples-title">Generated here, ready to try</h2><p>These examples use the installed model and the same presets available under Explore. “Use this example” in the studio restores the recorded inputs and seed.</p><div class="recipe-grid">{examples.map(example => <article class="recipe-card"><img class="pixel-art" src={'/studio/recipe-examples/'+example.id+'.webp'} width="1024" height="1024" loading="lazy" alt={example.subject}/><div class="recipe-card-body"><h3>{example.title}</h3><p>{example.note}</p><p><strong>Subject:</strong> {example.subject}</p><a href={'/studio/recipe-examples/'+example.id+'-pixels.png'} download>Download native pixel PNG ↓</a></div></article>)}</div></section>
<section class="guide"><div><h2>Preview and native PNG</h2><p>The model generates a large image. Atelier reduces its palette, picks the most common color in each grid cell, and expands the cleaned image with nearest-neighbor scaling. Both downloads contain the same cleaned pixels.</p><p>The large PNG is convenient to view and share. The native PNG is small enough to edit pixel by pixel. A 64 grid on a square canvas exports 64 × 64; a 16:9 canvas exports 64 × 36. Pixels stay square.</p></div><div><h3>Know the limits</h3><p>Cleanup makes a consistent grid and limits the palette. Check the actual drawing, outlines and anatomy yourself. The model can produce uneven clusters, stray details or inconsistent perspective. It does not guarantee tileable textures, coherent sprite animations or transparent backgrounds.</p><p>The native PNG is opaque RGB. For a sprite, use a plain contrasting background and remove it in a pixel editor. Export and resize with nearest-neighbor to keep the edges sharp.</p></div><div><h3>When the result feels busy</h3><p>Remove small props, simplify the silhouette and reduce the palette. Choose 128 for a detailed scene, rather than putting a long list of objects into a 32-pixel icon. Turbo is designed for eight steps; extra steps are not a reliable way to add detail.</p><p>Atelier’s deterministic palette and modal-cell cleanup is a separate implementation inspired by the author’s refiner. It does not include the original node’s automatic grid detector or perceptual color clustering.</p></div></section>
<section class="guide"><div><h2>Model and sources</h2><p><a href="https://huggingface.co/e-n-v-y/Krea-2-Pixel-Art" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">e-n-v-y · Krea 2 Pixel Art ↗</a></p><p>The pixel LoRAs use MIT. The base weights use the Krea 2 Community License; both apply. The complete model is not an MIT-only release.</p></div><div><p><a href="https://github.com/krea-ai/krea-2" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Krea · official Turbo and LoRA guidance ↗</a></p><p><a href="https://www.krea.ai/krea-2-licensing" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Krea 2 base-model license ↗</a></p></div><div><p><a href="https://github.com/envy-ai/ComfyUI-Krea2-Pixel-Art-Refiner" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Author’s original ComfyUI refiner ↗</a></p><p><a href="https://github.com/leejet/stable-diffusion.cpp/blob/master/docs/krea2.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Native runtime · Krea 2 support ↗</a></p></div></section>
</main></body></html>
